<section class='section'>
    <h3>What is Software Architecture?</h3>
    <p><strong>Software architecture</strong> is the high-level structure of a software system. It defines the major components, how they interact, and the technical rules that keep the system scalable, maintainable, secure, and reliable.</p>
</section>

<section class='section'>
    <h3>Why it matters</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Provides a clear blueprint before implementation starts.</li>
        <li>Helps teams scale features without rewriting everything.</li>
        <li>Improves maintainability by separating concerns.</li>
        <li>Supports reliability and fault isolation in distributed systems.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section class='section'>
    <h3>Architecture in a Microservices context</h3>
    <p>For microservices, architecture defines service boundaries, communication patterns (HTTP/events), data ownership, resiliency strategy, and deployment model. Good architecture allows each service to evolve independently while still working as one system.</p>
</section>
